1
00:00:00,198 --> 00:00:01,721
Welcome to this explainer on

2
00:00:01,721 --> 00:00:04,902
the MyMusicBox 5-Week
Repertoire Training System.

3
00:00:04,902 --> 00:00:07,458
Today, we're going to break
down the exact framework

4
00:00:07,458 --> 00:00:09,958
you need to teach repertoire effectively.

5
00:00:09,958 --> 00:00:13,117
We'll be walking through it week
by week so you can clearly see

6
00:00:13,117 --> 00:00:16,226
how to help your students build
lasting, solid musical skills.

7
00:00:16,966 --> 00:00:19,046
Let's just dive right into the syllabus.

8
00:00:19,046 --> 00:00:21,574
We'll be looking at five key areas today.

9
00:00:21,574 --> 00:00:22,918
The Five Foundations.

10
00:00:22,918 --> 00:00:25,766
The difference between
performance and practice pieces.

11
00:00:25,766 --> 00:00:27,430
The 5-week progression itself.

12
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:31,554
Some song-by-song examples, and
finally, your core teaching principles.

13
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:33,954
The Five Foundations.

14
00:00:34,758 --> 00:00:37,734
These are your absolute core
tools. Every single lesson you

15
00:00:37,734 --> 00:00:41,158
teach is built on one or more
of these sequential skills.

16
00:00:41,158 --> 00:00:43,878
It all starts with listening,
which trains the ear.

17
00:00:43,878 --> 00:00:48,066
Then we add movement, using off-the-piano
coordination to prep the body and mind.

18
00:00:48,742 --> 00:00:49,602
Third is singing.

19
00:00:50,310 --> 00:00:52,802
Singing helps them internalize the pitch
before a student ever touches a key.

20
00:00:53,478 --> 00:00:55,718
4th is actually playing at the keyboard.

21
00:00:55,718 --> 00:00:59,106
And finally, reading, where they
learn to read music step by step.

22
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:02,702
Now you definitely need to
adjust these foundations to

23
00:01:02,702 --> 00:01:05,670
fit the dynamic of your
specific class each week.

24
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:07,970
There is one strict timing
rule you need to follow.

25
00:01:08,614 --> 00:01:11,910
Physical playing has to
start no later than week 3.

26
00:01:11,910 --> 00:01:13,378
It's a critical milestone
in this curriculum.

27
00:01:14,406 --> 00:01:17,730
Let's move on to section 2, where
we categorize the repertoire

28
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:19,874
into performance versus practice pieces.

29
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:24,578
The way you identify these pieces
directly dictates your Week 1 lesson plan.

30
00:01:25,382 --> 00:01:28,998
Performance pieces have a rainbow
border, like the song "Bears."

31
00:01:28,998 --> 00:01:32,678
And for these, week one is
strictly listening and singing.

32
00:01:32,678 --> 00:01:34,086
No playing at all.

33
00:01:34,086 --> 00:01:37,794
On the flip side, practice pieces have
a solid color border, like "Cruising."

34
00:01:38,406 --> 00:01:40,898
For these ones, your students are
going to sing and play right away.

35
00:01:41,958 --> 00:01:43,744
Section 3: the five-week
progression itself.

36
00:01:43,744 --> 00:01:45,570
Let's see how these
skills map out over time.

37
00:01:46,534 --> 00:01:49,206
If you take a look at this
matrix, you'll see a standardized

38
00:01:49,206 --> 00:01:52,102
roadmap for a piece from
week one through week five.

39
00:01:52,102 --> 00:01:55,238
Notice those little colored rings
at the top left of each column?

40
00:01:55,238 --> 00:01:57,888
They give you a precise
visual cue for exactly which

41
00:01:57,888 --> 00:02:00,130
foundational skills are
activated that week.

42
00:02:00,934 --> 00:02:03,718
We start with listening
and movement in week one.

43
00:02:03,718 --> 00:02:06,502
By week 2, we're adding
in singing and playing.

44
00:02:06,502 --> 00:02:10,694
Week 3 is when we introduce reading,
using the magnet board and staff.

45
00:02:10,694 --> 00:02:14,534
By week 4, the focus shifts heavily
onto reading the Grand Staff.

46
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:17,120
And then it culminates in week
5, where your students play

47
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:19,394
the entire song along with
the accompaniment music.

48
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:24,006
Let's see what this looks like
in practice with section 4.

49
00:02:24,006 --> 00:02:25,378
Song-by-song examples.

50
00:02:26,246 --> 00:02:30,278
Here, we can trace the exact weekly
execution for a few different pieces.

51
00:02:30,278 --> 00:02:33,350
For example, if you look at a
practice piece like "Bells,"

52
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:36,962
Week 1 starts with tapping a real
bell and playing Do on the thumb.

53
00:02:37,606 --> 00:02:40,391
Compare that to a performance
piece like "Hammock," where

54
00:02:40,391 --> 00:02:43,270
week one is kept entirely
to listening and singing.

55
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:45,863
You can see exactly how the
activities map directly to those

56
00:02:45,863 --> 00:02:47,938
colored foundation tags
we talked about earlier.

57
00:02:48,838 --> 00:02:51,618
Now you might be wondering what
happens when those five weeks are up.

58
00:02:52,422 --> 00:02:54,630
The training doesn't just abruptly stop.

59
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:57,446
The songs enter a phase we call "5+".

60
00:02:57,446 --> 00:02:59,454
Basically, they stay in
the lesson for review as

61
00:02:59,454 --> 00:03:01,958
long as they're actively
helping the students.

62
00:03:01,958 --> 00:03:04,495
This is when your classes can
work on advanced coordination,

63
00:03:04,495 --> 00:03:06,440
like playing with both
hands, or maybe singing

64
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:08,258
with one hand while
playing with the other.

65
00:03:09,126 --> 00:03:12,742
And finally, section five,
the core teaching principles.

66
00:03:12,742 --> 00:03:15,778
Let's walk through the four overarching
pedagogical rules you'll use.

67
00:03:16,614 --> 00:03:19,234
Principle number one is
"voice before fingers".

68
00:03:19,846 --> 00:03:22,758
Vocal internalization
must always happen first.

69
00:03:22,758 --> 00:03:25,839
You sing a new part with the students,
and they only attempt to actually

70
00:03:25,839 --> 00:03:28,578
play it on the keyboard once they
securely know that vocal part.

71
00:03:29,702 --> 00:03:32,294
The second principle is "small steps".

72
00:03:32,294 --> 00:03:34,377
Every single week, you're
just adding one new

73
00:03:34,377 --> 00:03:36,838
thing to what the class already does well.

74
00:03:36,838 --> 00:03:40,411
It's a highly systematic approach
that builds confidence and ensures

75
00:03:40,411 --> 00:03:43,458
no one gets overwhelmed by a
sudden flood of new material.

76
00:03:44,486 --> 00:03:47,174
Principle 3 is "Parents as Partners".

77
00:03:47,174 --> 00:03:49,519
In this curriculum, active
parental involvement

78
00:03:49,519 --> 00:03:52,102
isn't just a "nice to
have." It's essential.

79
00:03:52,102 --> 00:03:54,308
You need parents pointing
to the music, echoing,

80
00:03:54,308 --> 00:03:56,008
and playing the games alongside their

81
00:03:56,008 --> 00:03:58,306
child to really support
that learning environment.

82
00:03:59,174 --> 00:04:01,638
And the fourth principle, "Songs overlap,"

83
00:04:01,638 --> 00:04:04,422
This is really about the
day-to-day reality of your classes.

84
00:04:04,422 --> 00:04:08,134
You're rarely going to be teaching
just one week's material in isolation.

85
00:04:08,134 --> 00:04:11,142
A new song will start, while
older songs are still growing.

86
00:04:11,142 --> 00:04:13,702
Which means every lesson
is a mix of weeks.

87
00:04:13,702 --> 00:04:16,026
You'll be managing multiple
pieces at different stages

88
00:04:16,026 --> 00:04:18,178
of the five-week progression
all at the same time.

89
00:04:19,398 --> 00:04:22,758
As you implement this structured
system, you have to ask yourself...

90
00:04:22,758 --> 00:04:25,446
How will you adjust these
foundations on the fly?

91
00:04:25,446 --> 00:04:27,586
Because every single class
is uniquely different.

92
00:04:28,262 --> 00:04:30,801
You have the structure and
you have the progression,

93
00:04:30,801 --> 00:04:33,681
but observing your students
and adapting these tools to fit

94
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:36,610
their specific needs is what
actually makes the system work.

95
00:04:37,606 --> 00:04:39,447
How will you tailor this
framework for the students

96
00:04:39,447 --> 00:04:40,674
sitting in front of you next week?
